          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            What is known as
            %em (Grothendieck’s) six operations
            is a formalization of structure that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                assigns to every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                of suitable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
                a (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+direct+image"}> derived
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"}> direct image
                \/(
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+inverse+image"}> derived
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %mo ⊣
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f^\ast \dashv f_*)
            %li
              %p
                assigns to every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+morphism"} separated morphism
                a
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image+with+compact+support"} direct image with compact support
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier dual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                        %mo ⊣
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_! \dashv f^!)
          %p
            These are
            %em four operations
            and together with
          %ul
            %li
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((-)\otimes X \dashv [X,-])
          %p
            form
            = succeed "." do
              %em six operations
          %p
            (All this is usually interpreted as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-functors"} (infinity,1)-functors
            so that for instance in the usual application to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+categories"} derived categories
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaves"} abelian sheaves
            the last two operations are really
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tor"} Tor
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ext"} Ext
          %p
            With a list of compatibility conditions between these (for instance (
            %a{:href => "#CisinskiDeglise09"}> Cisinski-Déglise 09, p. x
            ) this is a structure of
            = succeed "." do
              %em Grothendieck’s six operations
          %p These consistency conditions include the following:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The adjunctions are functorial, hence form
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functors"} 2-functors
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ↦
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mapsto f_*
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ↦
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mapsto f_!
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! \to f_*
                which is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalence"} natural equivalence
                when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"} proper map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
                (base change formulas): given a (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"}> homotopy
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi Q
                                %mn 1
                              %munder
                                %mo ×
                                %mi X
                              %msub
                                %mi Q
                                %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi p
                                      %mn 1
                              %mo ↙
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi p
                                      %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi Q
                                %mn 1
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi Q
                                %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mi f
                              %mo ↘
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mo ↙
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mi g
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      \&amp;&amp; Q_1 \underset{X}{\times} Q_2
                      \\\
                      \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{p_1}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{p_2}}
                      \\\
                      Q_1 &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; Q_2
                      \\\
                      \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{f}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{g}}
                      \\\
                      \&amp;&amp; X
                      }
              %p
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+morphism"} separated morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalences"} natural equivalences
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo !
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      g^\ast \circ f_! \simeq (p_2)_! \circ (p_1)^\ast
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo !
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      f^! \circ g_\ast \simeq (p_1)_\ast\circ (p_2)^!
                      \\,.
            %li
              %p …
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CisinskiDeglise09"}> Cisinski-Déglise 09, p. x
            )
          %p
            Morover one imposes a formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
            with
            = succeed "\u2026" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#CisinskiDeglise09"}> Cisinski-Déglise 09, p. xi
            )
          %h3#specializations Specializations
          %p Often specializations of the general concept play a role:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+context"}> Wirthmüller context
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≃
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^! \simeq f^\ast
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
                is a strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+functor"} closed monoidal functor
                (hence the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
                holds);
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfer+context"}> transfer context
                  \: in addition the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+formula"} projection formula
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
                  holds;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier-Grothendieck+context"}> Verdier-Grothendieck context
                \: the “projection formula”
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \otimes f_! X \simeq f_!(f^\ast Y \otimes X)
                holds naturally in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+context"}> Grothendieck context
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_! \simeq f_\ast
                and the projection formula holds
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                        %mi X
                        %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \otimes f_! X \simeq f_!(f^\ast Y \otimes X)
          %h2#manns_approach Mann’s approach
          %p
            Mann (
            %a{:href => "#Mann22"}> Mann 22
            ) has developed an abstract framework for a six-functor formalism that combines the approaches of Liu-Zheng and Gaitsgory-Rozenblyum and applies it to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+mathematics"} condensed mathematics
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+mathematics"} almost mathematics
            to construct an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-category
            of derived solid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X}^{+}/\pi
            almost modules (see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Scholze22"} Scholze22
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be an
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              be a class of morphisms stable under pullback and composition, and including all isomorphisms. The symmetric monoidal
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Corr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Corr}(C,E)
              has as its objects the objects in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a morphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is given by an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              together with maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi W
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mo
                                  %mi f
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mo
                                  %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; W
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \mathllap{^f}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow\mathrlap{^g}
                    \\\
                    X &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; Y
                    }
            %p
              where
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\in E
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cat
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cat}_{\infty}
              be the
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "-categories." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %em 3-functor formalism
              is a lax symmetric monoidal functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo :
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Corr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cat
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}:\mathrm{Corr}(C,E)\to \mathrm{Cat}_{\infty}
            %p
          %p
            The above definition formalizes three of the functors in the 6-functor formalism. The derived tensor product comes from the lax symmetric monoidal structure,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{*}
            comes from the image of the correspondence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi f
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\xleftarrow{f}X=X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{!}
            comes from the image of the correspondence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=X\xrightarrow{f}Y
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em 6-functor formalism
              is a 3-functor formalism for which
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\otimes A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{*}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{!}
              admit right adjoints.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_motivic_homotopy_theory Relation to motivic homotopy theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C2%29-category"} (infinity,2)-category
            of functors to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} stable (infinity,1)-categories
            which satisfy the
            %em six operations
            formalism (and a bit more, such that
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+theory"} A1-homotopy invariance
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+motivic+homotopy+theory"} stable motivic homotopy theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"}> Grothendieck duality
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
          %div
